   "id": "khZPrUxlCQAJ",
   "from": "Michael Hannon",
   "author": "d567d0ef3c054a35",
   "date": "2015-10-18T23:47:31Z",
   "subject": "Re: [D-RUG]: subsetting problem",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "You can test this on a fake data set.  Please have a look at the\r<br>appended.  Let me/us know if that does/does not help.  The result I\r<br>get from running it:\r<br>\r<br>&gt; cydno.rsem[chooseRows, ]\r<br>     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] [,5] [,6]\r<br>[1,]    0    0    0    0    1    1\r<br>[2,]    0    0    0    0    0    1\r<br>&gt;\r<br>\r<br>-- Mike\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>rows &lt;- 12\r<br>cols &lt;- 6\r<br>\r<br>set.seed(628)\r<br>\r<br>cydno.rsem &lt;- matrix(sample(c(0,1), rows * cols,\r<br>                            prob=c(0.6, 0.4), replace=TRUE), nrow=rows)\r<br>cydno.rsem\r<br>\r<br>males &lt;- 1:(cols / 2)\r<br>females &lt;- (cols / 2 + 1):cols\r<br>males\r<br>females\r<br>\r<br>chooseRows &lt;- apply(cydno.rsem, 1, function(nextRow) {\r<br>                        all(nextRow[males] == 0) &amp;\r<br>                            !all(nextRow[females] == 0)\r<br>                    })\r<br>cydno.rsem[chooseRows, ]\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Sun, Oct 18, 2015 at 4:25 PM, Christopher Hamm &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tophe...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Thanks Noam,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I was not clear. I wanted to make sure that at least one female had a value\r<br>&gt; in a row that had all males with 0’s.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Chris\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; On October 18, 2015 at 18:16:46, Noam Ross (<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">noam...@gmail.com</a>) wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Am I correct in that by “pull out”, you mean remove all the rows of males\r<br>&gt; that are all zeros? I think this will not quite do this, because if you have\r<br>&gt; non-zero females in the same row, that row will be left in. Rather, I think\r<br>&gt; you want\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; cyndo.rsem[rowSums(cydno.resm[1:10, ]) == 0, 1:10)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; On Sun, Oct 18, 2015 at 6:41 PM Christopher Hamm &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tophe...@gmail.com</a>&gt;\r<br>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen,\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; I am having a subsetting issue that I think I have solved but would like\r<br>&gt;&gt; someone to tell me I am wrong or right. I want to select only rows that have\r<br>&gt;&gt; ALL 0’s for males and values greater than 0 for at least one female.\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; I have a data.matrix (cydno.rsem) with 1000 rows and 20 columns. The first\r<br>&gt;&gt; 10 columns are males, and the last 10 columns are females. The data are\r<br>&gt;&gt; integers ranging from 0 to ~1000. It think this pull out all the rows of\r<br>&gt;&gt; males that are all 0’s:\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; cydno.rsem[rowSums(cydno.rsem != 0) == 0, 1:10]\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Am I correct?\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Thanks\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; Chris\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; --\r<br>&gt;&gt; Christopher Hamm\r<br>&gt;&gt; Postdoctoral Researcher\r<br>&gt;&gt; Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology\r<br>&gt;&gt; University of Kansas\r<br>&gt;&gt; 5032 Haworth Hall\r<br>&gt;&gt; 1200 Sunnyside Avenue\r<br>&gt;&gt; Lawrence, Kansas 66045-7566\r<br>&gt;&gt; USA\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; email: <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ch...@ku.edu</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; phone: <a href=\"tel:(785)%20864-3848\" value=\"+17858643848\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">(785) 864-3848</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; <a href=\"http://butterflyology.net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://butterflyology.net&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467741157000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0dvrM7YaEoXoZuG0prf1NC\">butterflyology.net</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; <a href=\"http://pizzabeerandscience.blogspot.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://pizzabeerandscience.blogspot.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467741157000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Wy41hCdeWPC5KtWU11sXD\">pizzabeerandscience.blogspot.com</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; @butterflyology\r<br>&gt;&gt; --\r<br>&gt;&gt; Check out our R resources at\r<br>&gt;&gt; <a href=\"http://www.noamross.net/davis-r-users-group.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.noamross.net/davis-r-users-group.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467741157000&amp;usg=AOvVaw19Fr6-cjoEci_DhyidsoWp\">http://www.noamross.net/davis-r-users-group.html</a>\r<br>&gt;&gt; ---\r<br>&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r<br>&gt;&gt; &quot;Davis R Users&#39; Group&quot; group.\r<br>&gt;&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r<br>&gt;&gt; email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">davis-rug+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt;&gt; Visit this group at <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/davis-rug\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http: